# Adds mythology ("The story"), circumpolar membership, Camelopardalis, and the
# Circumpolar intro to constellations.html. Idempotent.
import json, sys
sys.path.insert(0,'/tmp/claude-0/build'); import const_rey
P='/home/claude/work/v2/constellations.html'
MYTH={
'orion':"A giant hunter, son of Poseidon, who boasted he could kill every animal on Earth. Gaia sent a scorpion to stop him, and its sting killed him. Zeus set both in the sky on opposite sides, so Orion sinks in the west just as Scorpius rises in the east — the hunter forever fleeing his killer. To the Egyptians this was Sah, the soul of Osiris, the god who dies and returns.",
'scorpius':"The scorpion that killed Orion, sent by Gaia (in some tellings by Artemis) to punish the hunter’s boast. Placed on the far side of the sky, it rises only as Orion sets and sets as he rises, so the two barely share the sky. In Ovid, this same scorpion terrified the horses of the sun when Phaethon tried to drive his father’s chariot, sending the sun careening toward the Earth.",
'ursamajor':"Callisto, a huntress in Artemis’ band, loved by Zeus and turned into a bear — by jealous Hera, by Artemis, or by Zeus to hide her. Years later her son Arcas, out hunting, nearly speared her, and Zeus swept them both into the sky. Hera, still furious, persuaded the sea gods Oceanus and Tethys never to let the Bear bathe in their waters, which is why it never sets.",
'ursaminor':"Often said to be Arcas, Callisto’s son, set beside his mother; older tellings make it Cynosura, a nymph who nursed the infant Zeus on Crete. The Greeks noted that Phoenician sailors steered by the Little Bear while Greeks used the Great Bear. Polaris has marked north for only the last two thousand years or so; the pole slowly wanders.",
'cassiopeia':"Queen of Aethiopia, wife of Cepheus, who boasted that she (or her daughter Andromeda) was more beautiful than the sea nymphs. Poseidon sent the monster Cetus to ravage the coast, and Andromeda was chained to a sea cliff as a sacrifice. Cassiopeia was set in the sky on her throne and, as punishment, circles the pole — spending part of every night hanging upside down.",
'cepheus':"King of Aethiopia, husband of Cassiopeia and father of Andromeda. On an oracle’s advice he agreed to chain his daughter to the rocks for the sea monster. The whole family drama is in the sky together: Cepheus, Cassiopeia, Andromeda, her rescuer Perseus, the winged horse Pegasus and the monster Cetus.",
'andromeda':"The princess chained to a sea cliff as an offering to Cetus, to atone for her mother’s boast. Perseus, flying home after slaying Medusa, saw her, killed the monster and married her. She lies between her mother Cassiopeia and the hero, arms outstretched, still chained.",
'perseus':"The hero who beheaded the Gorgon Medusa, whose gaze turned people to stone, by watching her in a polished shield. Flying home on winged sandals, he rescued Andromeda from Cetus. In the sky he holds Medusa’s head: the star Algol, the ‘Demon Star’, is her eye, and it really does wink, dimming every 2.9 days.",
'pegasus':"The winged horse that sprang from Medusa’s neck when Perseus beheaded her. The hero Bellerophon tamed him with a golden bridle from Athena and rode him against the Chimera, but when he tried to fly to Olympus, Zeus sent a gadfly to sting Pegasus and Bellerophon fell. Pegasus flew on to the stars; only his front half shows, upside down.",
'cetus':"The sea monster Poseidon sent to ravage Aethiopia after Cassiopeia’s boast, killed by Perseus as it came for Andromeda. Drawn as a whale or sea serpent, it swims in the watery region of the sky with Pisces, Aquarius and Capricornus.",
'taurus':"Zeus in disguise as a gleaming white bull, who charmed the Phoenician princess Europa onto his back and swam with her to Crete, where she became mother of King Minos. Only the bull’s front half rises from the waves. The V of the Hyades is its face, red Aldebaran its eye, and the Pleiades, the Seven Sisters, ride on its shoulder.",
'gemini':"The twins Castor and Pollux, brothers of Helen of Troy. Pollux, son of Zeus, was immortal; Castor was not. When Castor was killed, Pollux begged to share his immortality, and Zeus let the pair spend alternate days on Olympus and in the underworld — always together. Sailors prayed to them for calm seas.",
'canismajor':"The greater of Orion’s two hunting dogs, at his heel. Its star Sirius, the Dog Star, gave us ‘the dog days’: to the Greeks, its rising with the Sun in high summer brought the season’s heat. In Egypt Sirius was Sopdet, whose return before dawn announced the Nile flood and the new year.",
'canisminor':"Orion’s smaller dog, trotting ahead of him. Its star Procyon means ‘before the dog’: it rises shortly before Sirius.",
'auriga':"The charioteer, usually Erichthonius, a king of Athens said to have invented the four-horse chariot. On his shoulder he carries the she-goat Amalthea, who nursed the infant Zeus — Capella means ‘little she-goat’ — and the small triangle of stars beside her are her kids.",
'leo':"The Nemean Lion, whose hide no weapon could pierce. Killing it was the first of the twelve labors of Heracles, who strangled it with his bare hands and wore its skin as armor. The backwards question mark of the Sickle is the lion’s mane.",
'virgo':"The maiden, usually Demeter, goddess of the harvest, or her daughter Persephone — Spica is the ear of wheat in her hand. Another tale makes her Astraea, goddess of justice, the last immortal to leave the Earth as people grew wicked; the scales of Libra beside her are hers.",
'bootes':"The herdsman, driving the Bears around the pole — Arcturus means ‘guardian of the bear’. Some say he is Arcas, following his mother Callisto forever; others make him Icarius, who learned winemaking from Dionysus and was killed by shepherds who thought he had poisoned them.",
'cygnus':"Zeus in the form of a swan, in which he visited Leda, queen of Sparta, mother of Helen and the twins Castor and Pollux. Another story makes the swan Cycnus, who dived again and again into the river Eridanus searching for his friend Phaethon, fallen from the sun’s chariot. The Swan flies down the Milky Way with its long neck stretched south.",
'lyra':"The lyre of Orpheus, made by Hermes from a tortoise shell. Orpheus played so beautifully that Hades let him lead his wife Eurydice back from the underworld — if he did not look back. He looked, and lost her. After his death the Muses asked Zeus to place his lyre among the stars.",
'aquila':"The eagle of Zeus, which carried his thunderbolts and swept the Trojan youth Ganymede up to Olympus to serve as cupbearer to the gods — Ganymede is Aquarius, pouring nearby. Altair, the eagle’s eye, is one corner of the Summer Triangle.",
'delphinus':"The dolphin Poseidon sent to find the sea nymph Amphitrite, who had fled his proposal. It persuaded her to return and marry him, and Poseidon set it in the sky in thanks. Another tale has a dolphin carry the poet Arion to safety after sailors threw him overboard.",
'scutum':"No ancient myth: Johannes Hevelius created it in 1684 as Sobieski’s Shield, honoring King John III Sobieski of Poland after his victory at the Siege of Vienna in 1683. It is one of only two constellations named after a real person.",
'sagittarius':"The archer, a centaur drawing his bow, his arrow aimed at Antares, the heart of the Scorpion. He is often said to be Crotus, son of Pan, who lived with the Muses on Mount Helicon and was credited with inventing archery. The center of our galaxy lies just beyond the tip of his bow.",
'ophiuchus':"The serpent bearer, Asclepius, god of medicine, holding a great snake (Serpens). He grew so skilled he could raise the dead, and Hades complained that the underworld was emptying, so Zeus struck him down with a thunderbolt and set him among the stars. His snake-wrapped staff is still the symbol of medicine.",
'serpens':"The great snake held by Ophiuchus, the healer Asclepius — snakes, which shed their skin and seem reborn, stood for renewal and healing. It is the only constellation split in two: the Head (Serpens Caput) and the Tail (Serpens Cauda), on either side of the serpent bearer.",
'hercules':"Heracles, the greatest Greek hero, son of Zeus, who performed twelve labors to atone for a madness sent by Hera. He kneels upside down in the sky with one foot on the head of Draco, the dragon Ladon he killed to take the golden apples of the Hesperides.",
'draco':"The dragon Ladon, who coiled around the tree of golden apples in the garden of the Hesperides and never slept, until Heracles killed him; Hera set him among the stars. Its star Thuban was the pole star when the Egyptians built the pyramids, about 4,500 years ago.",
'corvus':"Apollo’s raven, sent with a cup (Crater) to fetch water. It dawdled to eat figs, then came back with a water snake (Hydra) in its claws, blaming it for the delay. Apollo saw through the lie and set all three in the sky, the raven forever kept from the cup.",
'cancer':"The giant crab Karkinos, sent by Hera to nip at Heracles while he fought the many-headed Hydra. Heracles crushed it underfoot, and Hera set it in the sky for its loyalty. Its faint stars hold the Beehive Cluster, which the ancients called Praesepe, ‘the manger’.",
'aquarius':"The water bearer, Ganymede, a Trojan prince carried to Olympus by Zeus’ eagle to pour nectar for the gods. In Babylon and Egypt this part of the sky was linked to the rains and the Nile flood, which is why so many watery figures — Pisces, Cetus, Capricornus — gather around him in the region called the Sea.",
'capricornus':"The sea goat, linked to the god Pan, who leapt into the Nile to escape the monster Typhon: the half in the water became a fish, the half above stayed a goat. The goat-fish is far older than the Greeks — it was the emblem of Enki (Ea), the Babylonian god of fresh water and wisdom.",
'aries':"The golden ram that rescued the children Phrixus and Helle from their stepmother and flew them east. Helle fell into the sea (the Hellespont), but Phrixus reached Colchis. The ram’s golden fleece, guarded by a dragon, was the prize Jason and the Argonauts sailed to win.",
'triangulum':"The Greeks called it Deltoton, after the shape of the letter delta and of the Nile delta. Some said Zeus placed it there to honor the fertile land at the mouth of the Nile; others that it marks Sicily, the three-cornered island.",
'leoMinor':"No myth of its own: Johannes Hevelius drew it in 1687 from faint stars between Leo and the Great Bear — a lion cub filling a gap in the sky.",
'coronaborealis':"The crown of Ariadne, princess of Crete, who helped Theseus escape the Labyrinth with a ball of thread, only to be abandoned by him on the island of Naxos. The god Dionysus found her there and married her, and threw her wedding crown into the sky, where its jewels became stars.",
'camelopardalis':"No ancient myth: the Dutch mapmaker Petrus Plancius introduced it around 1613 to fill the dim gap near the pole. A ‘camelopard’ was the old name for a giraffe — a camel with a leopard’s spots. Plancius is said to have meant the camel that carried Rebecca to marry Isaac in Genesis.",
}
CAM={"key":"camelopardalis","name":"Camelopardalis","type":"Constellation","season":"Circumpolar","lead":"The faint giraffe of the north",
 "about":"A large, faint constellation filling the gap between Cassiopeia, Perseus and the Bears. Nothing in it is brighter than 4th magnitude, so it takes a dark sky — which Zion has.",
 "where":"Always above the northern horizon, wheeling around Polaris between Cassiopeia and Ursa Major. Highest on winter evenings.",
 "find":"Look in the empty-seeming patch between Capella and Polaris. Under a dark sky, a loose trail of faint stars runs from near Polaris down toward Capella and Perseus.",
 "objects":"Kemble’s Cascade, a chain of about 20 faint stars ending at the little cluster NGC 1502, is a lovely binocular sight. The spiral galaxy NGC 2403 is a good target for a long telephoto exposure.",
 "best":"All year (circumpolar); highest in winter","binocular":True,"stars":[],"lines":[]}
# Circumpolar from ~37 N: within ~53 deg of the pole. Kept in their season too.
ALSO={'ursamajor':'Spring','cassiopeia':'Autumn','cepheus':'Autumn','draco':'Summer','kemble':'Autumn'}
s=open(P).read(); i=s.index('const DATA = ')+13; data,end=json.JSONDecoder().raw_decode(s[i:])
if not any(e['key']=='camelopardalis' for e in data):
    st,ln,_=const_rey.build(CAM); CAM['stars']=st; CAM['lines']=ln
    k=[j for j,e in enumerate(data) if e['key']=='ursaminor'][0]; data.insert(k+1,CAM)
for e in data:
    if e['key'] in MYTH: e['myth']=MYTH[e['key']]
    if e['key'] in ALSO: e['season']='Circumpolar'; e['also']=[ALSO[e['key']]]
s=s[:i]+json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False)+s[i+end:]
open(P,'w').write(s); print('ok', len(data), sum(1 for e in data if 'myth' in e))
